From e03d5b1d4dfdd007e370cb45ad97d095ed4fb363 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stephen Trotter <33046718+sjtrotter@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 12:04:23 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 4/7] RDP: bind and own OAuth callback results

The WebKit OAuth flow accepted any redirect observed by the web view as
the authorization response, stored the borrowed URI string on the
protocol widget, and had the worker thread poll that pointer every 500 ms
without a bound. The request carried neither a state parameter nor PKCE.

Add a reference-counted transaction that owns the authorization URI, the
expected redirect, the state value, and the terminal result. The worker
that requests the token creates it and waits on a condition variable with
a five-minute deadline; the dialog and each idle hold their own reference
so callbacks always act on the transaction that created them, and the
protocol widget is kept alive by the transaction. A redirect completes the
transaction only when its scheme, host, port, and path equal the expected
redirect, it carries no userinfo or fragment, its state matches, and it
has exactly one of "code" or "error". Exactly one terminal result is
recorded; later redirects, window closes, or timeouts are ignored, and
closing from the worker destroys any live dialog on the GTK thread and
waits, bounded, for that to happen.

Requests now include a random state value and a PKCE S256 challenge, and
the verifier is sent with the token request. The authorization URI is
only loaded when it is an https URI whose host is the authority of a
cloud in the table, so the sign-in window and the token request accept
the same set of authorities. The "delete-event" handler returns a
gboolean as GTK expects.

The file is added to po/POTFILES.in: its dialog title has been marked for
translation since it was introduced, but the file was never listed, so
the string was not extracted.

Tested with FreeRDP 3.30.0. The series requires FreeRDP 3.16.0 or
newer: FreeRDP_GatewayAvdScope and FreeRDP_GatewayAvdAccessAadFormat
were added in that release, and every other API used is older.

This change was prepared with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic) and was
reviewed and tested by the author.
